### 

A recurrent theme in the literature is that households face significant upfront capital requirements and uncertainty about the financial returns of rooftop solar, which together deter adoption. Multiple studies list high initial costs, lengthy payback horizons, unclear return-on-investment prospects, and constrained access to borrowing as core financial obstacles to household uptake. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Empirical evidence links affordability concerns not only to absolute cost but also to limited awareness of financing options. In one survey, among households that recognized rooftop solar as expensive yet were not interested in adopting, 73 per cent reported they did not know about available finance pathways. {{cite:S1#c1}} This pattern implies that gaps in financial literacy and knowledge about lending or payment structures can be as decisive for adoption decisions as subsidy levels themselves. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Qualitative work from Karnataka reinforces the role of perceived financial risk alongside procedural frictions: households do not generally reject solar technology outright, but accumulated frictions throughout the adoption journey — including uncertainty about costs and returns — cause many to stall before installation. {{cite:S1#c6}} The Karnataka respondents describe the adoption pathway as procedurally heavy and institutionally slow, which amplifies perceived economic risk and reduces the likelihood that interest converts into purchase. {{cite:S1#c7}} 

Policy design therefore shapes financial attractiveness in two contrasting ways. On the one hand, subsidy schemes can materially cut initial outlays (for example, schemes that provide substantial support for larger systems). {{cite:S1#c1}} On the other hand, subsidy-related procedures can add complexity that raises effective transaction costs for households, creating a "subsidy paradox" in which the support mechanism itself becomes an adoption friction. {{cite:S1#c3}} 

Cross-state analysis further complicates a simple cost-driven account: inter-state variation shows that some states with modest nominal incentives outperform higher-subsidy states, and statistical tests find a weak—or even negative—relationship between subsidy magnitude and installation counts. {{cite:S2#c8}} This suggests that the manner in which financial support is delivered, the transparency and simplicity of its access, and complementary measures (for example, financing outreach) are critical to converting incentives into installations. {{cite:S2#c8}}{{cite:S1#c3}} 

Interventions aimed at reducing the financial barrier therefore combine two strands: make the economics clearer to households, and cut procedural transaction costs that magnify perceived risk. Studies recommend targeted financial-literacy tools and standardized "solar finance" materials to present EMI options, payback calculators, and bill-savings estimates; they also propose simplifying subsidy application routes and enabling direct end-user access to reduce vendor-mediated friction. {{cite:S1#c2}}{{cite:S1#c3}}

