### Financial constraints

Financial considerations emerge repeatedly as a core barrier to rooftop solar uptake, centered on high initial capital requirements, uncertain returns, and limited access to suitable finance. Bagla (2026) summarises prior studies identifying large upfront costs and long payback horizons as primary deterrents for household adoption. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Household perceptions of affordability often reflect gaps in knowledge about financing instruments as much as raw price sensitivity. Bagla (2026) reports evidence that many households who consider rooftop solar “expensive” are simply unaware of existing financing options; in one survey subset, 73 per cent of households who perceived systems as costly did not know about available finance schemes. {{cite:S1#c1}} This pattern implies that improving financial literacy and signposting of lending products can change perceived affordability without altering hardware prices. {{cite:S1#c2}}  

Policy design alters the economics of rooftop installations, but design choices can both help and hinder uptake. Bagla (2026) notes that the PM Surya Ghar subsidy—up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above—reduces the upfront burden for eligible systems, yet regulatory requirements such as ALMM and domestic-content rules can raise equipment costs and undercut those benefits for price-sensitive households. {{cite:S1#c1}} At the same time, Bagla (2026) identifies a “subsidy paradox” in which subsidy delivery mechanisms introduce procedural friction that can negate subsidy advantages by increasing delay, complexity, or vendor mediation. {{cite:S1#c3}}  

Empirical patterns at state and national levels support the conclusion that nominal subsidy levels are not a sufficient predictor of deployment. A cross-state analysis finds weak or counterintuitive associations between financial incentives and installation rates, including a negative correlation between subsidy percentage and adoption in some regressions, implying that incentive design, delivery quality, and administrative reliability influence outcomes more than headline subsidy amounts. {{cite:S2#c8}}  

Qualitative evidence from Karnataka situates these national patterns at household scale: even where policy frameworks are favourable, perceived financial risk combines with procedural and informational gaps to stall projects before initiation. Bagla (2026) reports that respondents in Karnataka experience perceived financial uncertainty alongside institutional delays and documentation hurdles, and that these compounded frictions help explain the low conversion from awareness to completed installation. {{cite:S1#c6}}{{cite:S1#c7}}  

Recommendations emerging from the literature therefore emphasise non-price remedies to financial barriers: targeted financial-literacy tools (for example, standardised calculators and EMI comparisons), deeper partnerships with lenders to widen access, and trialling financing models geared to low-consumption or shared-use households. Bagla (2026) suggests testing a “Solar Finance Kit” and stronger bank/microfinance linkages to increase uptake. {{cite:S1#c2}}

