### Financial constraints

Multiple studies identify upfront capital requirements and uncertainty over returns as central financial barriers to household rooftop solar adoption. The literature lists high initial cost, lengthy payback horizons, unclear return-on-investment prospects, and limited access to suited finance as recurrent deterrents cited by potential adopters. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Survey and qualitative evidence indicate that affordability concerns often reflect limited awareness of financing alternatives as much as outright price. In the CEEW sample reported here, a large majority of households that perceived rooftop solar as expensive were unaware of available financing options; among those who were aware but uninterested, 73 per cent lacked knowledge of such options. {{cite:S1#c1}} This pattern appears in the Karnataka interview data as well: many households stop short of initiation not because they reject the technology but because perceived financial risk and the perceived complexity of arranging payment create a halt in the adoption pathway. {{cite:S1#c6}}  

Policy features that on paper improve affordability can nonetheless have ambiguous effects once implementation frictions are included. Central subsidies such as PM Surya Ghar—which provide up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above—reduce headline investment needs, but parallel regulatory requirements and procurement rules (for example, domestic content and ALMM conditions) can raise equipment costs and thus blunt the subsidy’s net benefit to price-sensitive households. {{cite:S1#c1}} At the same time, cross-state empirical analysis finds a weak or even negative statistical relationship between nominal subsidy generosity and observed rooftop installation rates, suggesting that subsidy size alone does not reliably predict uptake. {{cite:S2#c8}}  

A related theme is the “subsidy paradox”: the design and delivery of subsidy support may introduce procedural burden that increases rather than reduces friction for households seeking finance. Evidence from Karnataka and national comparisons shows that complex, vendor-mediated subsidy application processes, lengthy approval waits, and opaque steps around net-metering and documentation deter completion even among motivated households. {{cite:S1#c3}}{{cite:S1#c7}}  

Several empirical and evaluative studies therefore treat financial constraints as a compound problem of liquidity plus information and procedure. Recommendations drawn from the same evidence base include interventions to boost financial literacy—standardised tools such as EMI calculators and payback estimators—and to expand formal lending linkages with banks and microfinance institutions to widen financing uptake for low-consumption households. {{cite:S1#c2}}{{cite:S1#c1}} These measures aim to shift the barrier set from one of perceived unaffordability to one of manageable financing and clearer subsidy access. {{cite:S1#c2}}{{cite:S1#c6}}

