Design and delivery of subsidies affect perceived cost-effectiveness in non-obvious ways. Central schemes can substantially lower nominal upfront outlays for qualifying systems, but regulatory requirements and rising component costs may erode those gains for price-sensitive buyers. {{cite:S1#c1}} Moreover, the administrative design of subsidy programs can introduce its own friction: when subsidy procedures are complex or vendor-mediated, they can increase delays and confusion, producing a “subsidy paradox” in which the support mechanism itself discourages uptake. {{cite:S1#c3}}

Household-level qualitative evidence from Karnataka illustrates how these financial frictions interact with procedural obstacles. The study sampled households that had adopted and those that had not, and it finds that friction accumulates across stages from consideration to installation, so even economically motivated households often drop out before completion. {{cite:S1#c6}} Respondents specifically mention delays from DISCOMs, complex documentation, and uncertainty about net-metering approvals as factors that amplify perceived financial risk and raise the effective cost of adoption. {{cite:S1#c7}}

