Policy subsidies reduce the nominal upfront cost of residential rooftop systems, but empirical evidence shows subsidies do not by themselves convert interest into installations. The literature frames adoption as constrained by multiple frictions—financial, informational, technical, regulatory and behavioural—so that subsidies create necessary but not sufficient conditions for uptake. {{cite:S1#c4}}

Household-level studies indicate that affordability concerns often reflect limited awareness of financing options rather than absolute price alone. A national survey found that among households who perceived rooftop solar as expensive yet were uninterested, 73 per cent did not know about available financing alternatives, signalling that financial literacy mediates how subsidy generosity translates into perceived affordability. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same review notes that policy design shapes financial attractiveness: for example, PM Surya Ghar provides up to INR 78,000 for systems of 3 kW and above, which can materially lower initial investment needs for eligible systems. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Field evidence from Karnataka corroborates these patterns while highlighting where subsidy delivery falters. In the state sample, adoption stalls because friction accumulates across the adoption stages, so even with a progressive policy framework many households drop out between awareness and installation. {{cite:S1#c6}} Respondents repeatedly reported delays and procedural complexity—such as slow DISCOM/BESCOM processes, cumbersome documentation, and uncertain net-metering timelines—that impede households from converting willingness into completed installations. {{cite:S1#c7}} This accumulation of institutional and procedural frictions shapes the household experience of subsidies: the barrier is not the absence of financial support but the "last mile" interface through which recipients must navigate. {{cite:S1#c4}}{{cite:S1#c7}}

Several studies identify a “subsidy paradox”: subsidy mechanisms can introduce additional administrative friction that increases, rather than reduces, barriers to uptake. Research suggests testing reforms such as enabling end-users to apply directly for subsidies, simplifying stepwise forms and checklists, and reassessing technical procurement requirements that raise equipment costs for residential systems. {{cite:S1#c3}} Complementary proposals advocate strengthening financial literacy and outreach—standardised finance information kits, lender partnerships, and demonstration sites—to address perceptional and procedural friction that subsidies alone do not resolve. {{cite:S1#c2}}

Cross-state analysis reinforces that higher nominal subsidy rates do not guarantee higher adoption: inter-state comparisons find weak links between installations and conventional economic predictors, and in some analyses a negative correlation between subsidy percentage and uptake. {{cite:S2#c8}} Together, these findings imply that the effectiveness of subsidy policy depends on delivery quality, transparency, and the surrounding information and institutional ecosystem rather than on headline subsidy levels alone. {{cite:S2#c8}}{{cite:S1#c1}}

